    "q-158420": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158420",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Compute $P(3<X<4)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2},\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}(x-1) & 1<x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X<4) & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x]}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}([\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2}-(4)]-[\\frac{1}{2}(3)^{2}-(3)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(8-4-\\frac{9}{2}+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
